Some things it lets you do:
  - Levitate over a pool while you #dip things in it.
  - Cross the giant lake on the 3.1 Medusa level.
  - Get into Fort Ludios in 3.1.
  - Avoid all the trapdoors in the Castle.
  - Get out of pits quickly or avoid them entirely.
  - Cross the water that surrounds the wizard's hiding place, the entry
    to the Sanctum in 3.1, etc.
  - Control your movements on the Air and Water elemental planes in 3.1.
  - Levitate over an "Elbereth" engraving so you don't scuff it out.
  - Float over and trigger a land mine without damaging your legs
    (but you still lose some HP).
  - Allow you to screw up and move over lava or water without killing
yourself.

There are other ways to do most of these things (spellbook of levitation,
wands of cold for freezing water, etc.), but a ring of levitation is a lot
more convenient, and it can't run out like a wand or spell.

> ->"blessed luckstone"
> It's fairly obvious why this is good, but... what does luck _do_?

Luck gets used in a lot of calculations in NetHack.  A few that I can think
of off the top of my head are:
  - Your chance of hitting monsters.  This is a chance out of 20, so if
    your luck is +10 or so, it's a big help.
  - The damage you do when you hit (I think?).
  - Your chance of successfully casting a spell.

> ->"blessed ring of conflict"
> Why on _earth_?...

In NH 3.0 a ring of conflict is one of the most powerful items in the game.
 When you're wearing it, monsters will attack each other instead of
attacking you.  So the ring is very handy for dealing with hordes of hill
orcs, throne rooms, zoos, the Castle, etc.  It also comes in handy with the
wizard summons lots of nasty pets to beat you up.  The down side is that
you don't get the experience points when monsters kill each other.  But
after experience level 15 or so, the points don't really matter very much
anyway.

In NH 3.1 the ring isn't quite as powerful.  Monsters still attack each
other, but they can attack you too.  It's still fairly useful, especially
if your armor class isn't that great.  In my last game, a Wizard with an AC
of -60 (!), I had a ring of conflict but never needed to use it.

